@page "/about"

<h1>About / Documentation</h1>
<div class="card">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h2>Purpose</h2>
        <p>MindConnect is a course MVP that helps patients manage mental health support appointments with psychologists.</p>

        <h2>Main features</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Register, login, and logout using ASP.NET Core Identity.</li>
            <li>Create patient or psychologist accounts.</li>
            <li>Psychologists can register their specialty, bio, available days, and available hours.</li>
            <li>Patients can schedule appointments only within psychologist availability.</li>
            <li>Patients can create, view, edit, cancel, delete, and review appointments.</li>
            <li>Psychologists can view and cancel appointments booked with them.</li>
            <li>The dashboard changes based on whether the user is a patient or psychologist.</li>
            <li>The app validates invalid login attempts, past dates, unavailable times, and duplicate appointment slots.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to use</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Create an account or use the seeded sample patient account.</li>
            <li>Use the Dashboard to review appointment summaries.</li>
            <li>Open Psychologists to review available providers and schedules.</li>
            <li>Use Schedule Appointment to create a valid appointment as a patient.</li>
            <li>Use My Appointments and History to manage records.</li>
            <li>Log in as a psychologist to view appointments booked with that psychologist.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Technologies</h2>
        <p>.NET 8, Blazor Server, EF Core, SQLite, ASP.NET Core Identity, and Bootstrap.</p>

        <h2>Data storage</h2>
        <p>The app stores users, psychologist profile fields, and appointments in a local SQLite database through Entity Framework Core.</p>

        <h2>Limitations and future improvements</h2>
        <p>Future versions could add a calendar-style scheduler, reminders, appointment search, admin reporting, and a production database such as PostgreSQL.</p>
    </div>
</div>
